# What never belongs in a build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that bites. The obvious one is size: the
# whole context is sent to the daemon before the first instruction runs, and a
# .venv or a .pgdata directory turns a two-second build into a two-minute one.
#
# The one that actually causes incidents: anything copied into an image is IN the
# image, and stays there in a layer even if a later instruction deletes it.
# A .env with a real password is then shipped to whoever can pull the tag.

# --- secrets and local state ------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.pgdata/
*.pem
*.key

# --- build and run output ---------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
artifacts/
allure-results/
allure-report/
test-results/
junit*.xml
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage

# --- version control and tooling --------------------------------------------
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.pre-commit-config.yaml
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- documentation ----------------------------------------------------------
# Not README.md: pyproject.toml declares it as the package readme, and the build
# fails at metadata generation without it. That exact mistake is recorded in
# Phase 2, with a pip error message that pointed nowhere near the cause.
docs/
LICENSE
